Hmm. The problem constraints are very big, up to 1e9 for N and M. So we can't compute F(i,j) for each cell individually and sum them up. We need a formula that can compute the sum efficiently, probably in O(1) per test case.

First, let's figure out what F(i,j) is for various (i,j). Since knight moves can vary, perhaps there's a pattern or formula for the minimal steps.

Let's recall how a knight moves. Each move changes the coordinates by (±1, ±2) or (±2, ±1). So, to reach (i,j), we can model this as a BFS problem. But since the chessboard is very big, we need to find a mathematical pattern for F(i,j).

